// Kircher's plate of the seventy-two names of God, each with its nation:
// Athanasius Kircher, Oedipus Aegyptiacus, vol. II part 1 (Rome, 1653), the
// fold-out headed "Iconismus inserendus tom. II Fol. 287", which p. 287
// introduces ("schema quod sequitur"). Lenain cites pp. 273 and 287 under his
// first cabalistic table, and the table's last two columns, the nation and
// its name of God, follow this plate's two outer rings row for row, from 1
// (Hebræi) to 72 (Thraces, KALO). See plan 033, "Other witnesses".
//
// The copy read is the National Library of Poland's, as Wikimedia Commons
// serves it, which Commons marks public domain:
//   file page  https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kircher-Diagram_of_the_names_of_God_(22593924).jpg
//   original   https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/60/Kircher-Diagram_of_the_names_of_God_%2822593924%29.jpg
//              a JPEG of 2926 × 4000 pixels, SHA-256
//              02d659c4e1326866bae618389de6baeef5ff9b121f4e57121faa116bc1ccbea6
//   Polona     https://polona.pl/item/athanasii-kircheri-soc-iesu-oedipi-aegyptiaci-tomus-t-2-ps-1-gymnasium-siue,OTAyNjM5Ng/289/
//              (the library's own viewer, image 289)
// It is mirrored in public/sources/ (src/sources/mirroredPages.ts,
// `kircherPlate`). Another copy, the Universidad Complutense de Madrid's
// (archive.org bub_gb_XjfzMnomDQoC, leaf n294), was photographed with the
// wheel's left part folded over.
//
// Read on 18 and 19 November 2026 from that original by a machine reader (Claude
// Opus 5.5, reasoning high) that was not told Lenain's table or entries, cell by cell
// from close crops and a polar unwrapping of the wheel about its centre.
//
// The alignment. Only the two outer rings have seventy-two cells: the names
// of God, and inside them the nations; their dividers line up. The numbers 1
// to 72 run clockwise, each engraved in the gap between two of the sun's
// spikes, over a divider, so the geometry alone does not say whether a
// number belongs to the cell clockwise of it or the one anticlockwise. Each
// was given to the cell clockwise of it. That puts 1 on Hebræi and 72 on
// Thraces, which is where the plate's own sequence breaks (…Bœotij,
// Phryges, Thraces | Hebræi, Syri, Chaldęi…), and it is the numbering
// Lenain's table follows row for row. The other reading would make Thraces
// the first.
//
// `godName` and `nation` are as engraved: ſ is the long s, ę the e with a
// hook, a tilde marks an omitted n, ij is kept, abbreviations and points are
// kept, and Greek, Hebrew, Syriac, Arabic and Armenian are in their own
// scripts, capitals and minuscules as cut. `confidence` is the letters'
// (medium or low; high where it is left out), not the alignment's. `note`
// says what the reader saw where it is not plain.
//
// Out of scope: the rings inside these two (Latin attributes of God, Hebrew
// letters numbered 1 to 42, single Hebrew letters) have forty-two cells, not
// seventy-two, do not follow the rays, and are not transcribed; nor is the
// twelve-pointed star at the centre or the trees around the wheel.
//
// `departures` is ours: each place where one of Lenain's entries departs
// from its ray, in the nation (`people`) or the name of God (`godName`),
// beyond transliteration. Transliteration is a different spelling of the
// same name in another alphabet or the same letters: V for U, Y for I, TH
// for T, a letter doubled or not (GOTT, Goth), a Greek name in roman
// letters (Θεὸς, Théos), the Tetragrammaton as Jehovah, الله as Allah,
// Θωντ as Teut or Theuth, and an alternative spelling Lenain adds with "ou"
// (Idio ou Iddio, Biub ou Biud). A second name joined with "et", a
// different word, a different people, or nothing at all is a departure.
// `gloss` says in English who Kircher's nation are; `cause` is a likely
// cause, given only where the letters show it; `tableAgrees` marks a
// departure of the entry alone, where Lenain's table, on both plates, has
// Kircher's nation. Each becomes an editorial
// note (scripts/seventyTwoAngels/apparatus.ts), with what the 1823 printing
// prints there from witnesses.json5, beside this file. Lenain's words stay
// the edition's data; the notes say what his source has.
//
// `nations` is ours too: every ray whose nation the entry does not depart
// from, with the entry's French beside Kircher's Latin, each pair accepted
// by hand as the same people (Galli, la France; Sinæ, les Chinois). A test
// holds every entry to this list or to `departures`, never both, so a new
// reading of either side that changes a nation fails. The 37th and 38th
// have no French: their entries name no people, but give the name as the
// philosophers' and the cabalists' (`remarks`).
//
// `remarks` are rows whose entry names no nation although it is not silent
// about whose name it is: the first table shows them beside the empty cell.
//
// A ray's `remark` is said beside its row in the first table: the
// engraver's slip on ray 1, and that ray 7's letters are a guess.
{
  rays: [
    {"ray": 1, "godName": "יהרה", "nation": "Hebræi", "confidence": "medium", "remark": "Engraved יהרה, with a resh where the Tetragrammaton has vav: the engraver's slip, recorded and not corrected.", "note": "Yod, he, a letter as wide as the he with no left leg, he: a resh where the Tetragrammaton has vav (ray 3's vav is narrower). The engraver's slip, recorded and not corrected; the alternative is a vav with an oversized head."},
    {"ray": 2, "godName": "ܐܠܗܐ", "nation": "Syri", "confidence": "medium", "note": "Syriac, crudely cut: read right to left as alaph, lamadh, he, alaph. The Unicode letters interpret the shapes."},
    {"ray": 3, "godName": "אלוה", "nation": "Chaldęi", "confidence": "medium", "note": "The first letter is Y-shaped, an alef without its lower-left foot (or a tsade without its base); the other three are clear."},
    {"ray": 4, "godName": "الله", "nation": "Arabes", "confidence": "medium", "note": "Rough Arabic, three uprights and a closed final loop, read alif, lam, lam, ha."},
    {"ray": 5, "godName": "Θωντ", "nation": "Ægyptij", "confidence": "medium", "note": "Θ, ω, a pointed letter drawn like ν but perhaps meant as υ (Θωυτ), τ."},
